s Corporations all the way back  to Daniel Webster who is one of the  greatest Supreme Court advocates ever  argued more than 220 cases in the  Supreme Court in the early 1800s many of  them on behalf of Business Corporations  and seeking constitutional protections  for business clients and so corporations  have always been able to one hire the  best lawyers that money can buy two  always had the appetite for risk  willingness to to pursue potentially  losing lawsuits because you know if you  can defeat a regulation that really  prevents you from making millions and  millions of dollars the fact that you  might spend a little bit of money  challenging that law in court it's just  the cost of doing business and so and  today for instance there's an elite  Supreme Court Bar in Washington that is  that studies show is much more effective  at getting their cases heard by the  Supreme Court and that those lawyers  today represent primarily Business  Corporations  one of the most interesting parts your  book is  this sort of liberal fight for for  corporations in the First Amendment it's  sort of stories that we don't really  think about when we think about Citizens  United but a lot of them come from  newspapers newspapers suing for their  free speech rights newspapers or  corporations New York Times Washington  Post if you will tell us that story I  mean it's like I think people forget  that like the the place where we get the  news also makes money that's right and  we the corporations it's not very  reductionist when you look back at this  history you find that actually liberals  will often arguing for the rights of  corporations and that I find actually  surprising limits on the rights of  corporation were imposed by the supreme  court in the notorious Lochner era a  period in the courts history where it  was very business-friendly but yet  nonetheless imposed limits on the rights  of corporations the story you asked  about with is really the story about  about the newspaper corporations who in  the 1930s took on Huey Long  Louisiana's iron-fisted demagogue who  was the governor in the senator and long  won the governorship of Louisiana on the  eve of the Great Depression promising to  make Louisiana great again and it's sort  of a Donald Trump thing he was Trump  before Trump and he really was he was a  populist who was great at using the  media and a showman and it news ya know  and in fact when the major newspapers in  Louisiana came out against Huey Long's  populism and his program he accused them  of publishing fake news and his allies  imposed attacks on newspaper  corporations on their advertising and  those newspapers challenged that tax in  court and won a landmark ruling one of  the very first Supreme Court rulings  striking down a law for violating the  freedom of the press the court said that  the free issued a broad opinion that's  still cited to this day for press  freedoms and the court said that  corporations have that right to that in  a modern democracy newspapers check  government and courage democratic  deliberation and they're published by  corporations that's an important  precedent from the 1930s that ends up  being cited by the Supreme Court  all the way including in citizens united  as establishing that corporations do  have free speech rights even when they  impact politics this is a devastating  security breach that was leaked out of  the Pentagon the most highly classified  documents of the war the time says 7,000  pages detailing how the White House has  been lying about the Vietnam War for 30  years the post this celebrated movie of  the past year that has liberals cheering  about the Washington Post standing up  for its First Amendment rights we should  remember that's a for-profit business  corporation standing up for its First  Amendment rights the right to engage in  political speech so it does show that  the story behind citizens united is a  complicated one and it's not just a  matter of sort of conservative  free-market principles applied to  campaign finance law as many people  suggest but actually it's a deeply  rooted issue that corporations have had  already long before Citizens United  established a toehold in American  political speech by using the  Constitution to expand their rights now  many people think about the  n-double-a-cp being a corporation it's a  people think of it as an association of  people but it definitely had to fight  for its free speech rights you talk  about a little bit in the book can you  talk about that here yeah well so for a  long time the Supreme Court said that  business corporations and corporations  generally do not have a freedom of  association and what happened in the  1950s is that after Brown versus Board  of Education there were southern states  that tried to put the n-double-a-cp out  of business stop them from filing  lawsuits in their states and I tell the  story about Alabama's effort to shut  down that n-double-a-cp  now of course the n-double-a-cp was not  a business but it turns out it was a  corporation and it's formed and  organized as a corporation under New  York law a nonprofit corporation but  takes the corporate form nonetheless and  Alabama tried to use the fact that it  was a corporation to put it out of  business to say that we have power to to  fight against this corporation and post  special rules on this corporation and  the corporation is no freedom of  association to hide its membership from  us for instance this  Court delivers an important victory for  the n-double-a-cp saying the  n-double-a-cp does even though it's a  corporation it does have certain  fundamental rights of association that  case would then be sided years later in  cases brought by Business Corporations  and indeed in Citizens United the  Supreme Court kind of envisions Business  Corporations to be like the  n-double-a-cp kind of voluntary  membership associations that have the  same kind of reason for people coming  