ited if corporations have the right to  spend as much money as they want to  influence elections but  cause of the unique ability of  corporations to amass capital it really  does give corporations extensively more  rights than individuals because it'd be  hard for very many individuals to match  the financing that say one company Apple  could put together if it wanted to  influence elections but we've seen at  least so far that corporations  especially the biggest biggest  brand-name corporations have often  stayed at the sidelines since citizens  united uncertain about whether this kind  of spending is a good idea for politics  or not but we're seeing corporations get  more and more involved in our political  lives look in the wake of the parklands  shooting we've seen a whole bunch of  businesses like Delta and Dick's  Sporting Goods and Walmart kind of take  on the NRA so these days we're expecting  more of our corporations to engage in  political life so maybe citizens united  is finding more of a home than people  realize are they staying on the  sidelines because of public shaming or a  public a public that might be  uncomfortable with them being an  election so much well I think we look  back in history what we see is that  often corporations have have seen  windows for them to be more active in  terms of political spending but they've  been hesitant to take advantage of those  windows often for fear of alienating  customers for feeling alienating workers  but we'll have to see what what time  will tell so far we've seen from  citizens united it's unleashed more  independent spending by individuals than  it has by Business Corporations we'll  have to see how that carries through in  the next few cycles well thank you very  much Adam we enjoyed talking to you  thank you for having me the book is  called we the corporations for reason  and poly-drug
 

